  "answer_to_user": "I screened for a 5-7% yield band with a payout-ratio ceiling of 70% (so the dividend isn't paying out more than the company can comfortably cover). Top results include Saul Centers (BFS.US) ~7.0% with a very low reported payout, Shinhan Seobu T&D REIT (404990.KO) ~6.98%, Huzhou Gas (6661.HK) ~6.97% at a ~64% payout, Fun Yours Technology (6482.TWO) ~6.96% at ~56% payout, Dashang Co (600694.CG) ~6.96% at ~69% payout, and HLA Group (600398.CG) ~6.95% at ~39% payout. The reason the payout ratio matters: a company earning $1 and paying $0.90 has little room to keep paying if earnings dip, whereas one paying $0.40 has a cushion — that's what separates a durable 6% from a 6% about to be cut. One important nuance the data itself flags: payout ratio is interpreted differently by sector. REITs (like the Saul Centers / Shinhan entries here) legally distribute most of their cash flow, so a 'high' payout on an earnings basis is normal for them — you'd look at FFO coverage rather than EPS payout. So these look safer than the trap-tier 40% yields from a pure ranking, but confirm each name's payout basis (REIT vs ordinary) and dividend history before treating any as safe. Not personalized advice."
